statement:
Let $\Omega$ be a finite measurable space — finite and discrete, so that every subset is measurable — and let $\mu$ be a finite measure on $\Omega$, writing $\mu^{\bbr}$ for its real-valued mass function. Then for every subset $S \subseteq \Omega$,
\[
  \mu^{\bbr}(S) \;=\; \sum_{\omega \in \Omega}
    \begin{cases} \mu^{\bbr}(\{\omega\}) & \text{if } \omega \in S, \\ 0 & \text{otherwise.} \end{cases}
\]
That is, the measure of $S$ is the sum of the masses of its individual points.
